<p>This page covers the forms that do not read as you expect, one entry each: the form, a fragment, and what it means.</p>

<h2 id="receivers">Methods that change their receiver</h2>

<h3 id="ref-this"><code>ref this</code></h3>
<pre><code class="byn">void record(ref this, i32 ms) {
    hits += 1
}</code></pre>
<p>A method is read-only by default; one that changes its receiver says so with <code>ref this</code> as its first parameter and cannot be called on a <code>let</code>.</p>
<example name="reading-ref-this"/>

<h3 id="with"><code>x with { … }</code></h3>
<pre><code class="byn">let b = a with { qty: 5, paid: true }</code></pre>
<p><code>with</code> copies a struct with some fields replaced, leaving the original untouched. It is the only way to copy with changes, and the listed fields are checked against the type's <code>requires</code> rules.</p>
<example name="reading-with"/>

<h2 id="context">Names taken from context</h2>

<h3 id="target-typed">Target-typed <code>.member</code></h3>
<pre><code class="byn">return .notFound
let c: Color = .red
fail .told if shouldFail</code></pre>
<p>A leading dot names a member of the type the compiler already expects: a union case, error variant or enum value. <code>.red</code> here is <code>Color.red</code>. It has one meaning only, never a lambda shortcut.</p>
<example name="reading-target"/>

<h3 id="it"><code>it</code></h3>
<pre><code class="byn">any(xs, by: it &gt; 2)
any(xs, by: x =&gt; x &gt; 5)</code></pre>
<p>Where a call expects a one-parameter function, an argument mentioning <code>it</code> is a lambda with <code>it</code> as its parameter; the two lines above mean the same. A nested second <code>it</code> is an error, so name one lambda. In a <code>requires</code> clause, <code>it</code> is the value being checked:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">string username
    requires it.runeCount in 3..=32</code></pre>
<example name="reading-it"/>

<h2 id="calls">Calls and parameters</h2>

<h3 id="particles">Particles in calls and parameters</h3>
<pre><code class="byn">void transfer(i32 amount, from Account payer, to Account payee)

transfer(200, from: aisha, to: maryam)</code></pre>
<p>A required parameter after the first takes a role word: before the type in the declaration, a label with a colon in the call. The words are <code>from to until through at by for as</code>. A call that leaves one out or writes the wrong one does not compile.</p>
<pre><code class="byn">i32 clamp(i32 x, to i32 max) =&gt; x &gt; max ? max : x

clamp(12, to: 10)</code></pre>

<h3 id="arrow">Bodies with <code>=&gt;</code></h3>
<pre><code class="byn">i32 square(i32 x) =&gt; x * x</code></pre>
<p><code>=&gt;</code> reads "evaluates to". A one-expression function body uses it in place of braces and <code>return</code>; it also separates a lambda's parameters from its body and a <code>switch</code> arm from its result. Function types use <code>-&gt;</code> instead: <code>i32 -&gt; bool</code>.</p>
<example name="reading-expression-body"/>

<h3 id="interpolation">Interpolated strings <code>$"…{…}"</code></h3>
<pre><code class="byn">print($"{name} has {name.length} bytes")
print($"{n, as: .hex}")</code></pre>
<p>A <code>$</code> before the quote lets you write expressions inside braces. A format option is a named argument inside the hole, like any other argument. Strings have no <code>+</code>, so this is also how you join them.</p>

<h2 id="statements">Statements</h2>

<h3 id="postfix-if"><code>stmt if cond</code></h3>
<pre><code class="byn">return if n &lt; 0
fail .outOfStock if line.qty &gt; stock
print("slow") unless fast</code></pre>
<p>A simple statement can end with <code>if</code> or <code>unless</code>, on the same line and only on a single simple statement. The condition is evaluated first, and the statement runs only when it holds.</p>

<h3 id="defer-failing"><code>defer failing</code></h3>
<pre><code class="byn">defer arena.reset()          // every exit
defer failing tx.rollback()  // only when the function fails</code></pre>
<p><code>defer</code> runs a statement when the scope ends, in reverse order. <code>defer failing</code> runs it only if the function exits by failing.</p>

<h2 id="errors">Errors</h2>

<h3 id="fails"><code>fails E</code></h3>
<pre><code class="byn">i32 digit(string s) fails ParseError {
    fail .empty if s.isEmpty
    …
}</code></pre>
<p><code>fails</code> after a signature lists the error sets the function can produce. <code>fail .empty</code> leaves with an error; <code>return</code> always returns a value. There are no exceptions.</p>

<h3 id="try-catch"><code>try</code> and <code>catch</code></h3>
<pre><code class="byn">let d = try digit(s)               // pass the failure up
let n = digit(s) catch -1          // fall back to a value
let m = digit(s) catch { return }  // leave
let k = digit(s) catch switch {
    .empty =&gt; 0,
    .notDigit =&gt; -1,
}</code></pre>
<p><code>try</code> propagates a failure to the caller and covers the whole expression to its right; <code>catch</code> handles it where it happens. See <a href="errors.html">Errors</a>.</p>
<example name="errors-basic"/>

<h3 id="except"><code>except</code></h3>
<pre><code class="byn">union error Retryable = CarrierError except .rejected

let q = send(n) catch (CarrierError.rejected) 1</code></pre>
<p><code>except</code> takes a pattern away from a set. On an error set it gives a narrower one: <code>Retryable</code> is every <code>CarrierError</code> but <code>.rejected</code>. <code>catch (CarrierError.rejected) 1</code> handles that one error with a value, so <code>retry</code> may declare <code>fails Retryable</code> and nothing else; the compiler checks that nothing else escapes.</p>
<example name="reading-except"/>
<note>Planned: <code>except</code> on a sequence (<code>for x in xs except null</code>, <code>users except null</code>) is specified but not in the compiler yet; it subtracts a pattern and narrows the loop variable.</note>

<h2 id="effects-ownership">Effects and ownership</h2>

<h3 id="uses"><code>uses</code></h3>
<pre><code class="byn">public List&lt;i32&gt; squares(i32 n) uses alloc { … }</code></pre>
<p><code>uses</code> lists a function's effects: <code>alloc suspend net fs clock random env process console</code>. No <code>uses</code> means pure. A public function that does something it did not declare does not compile.</p>

<h3 id="move"><code>move</code></h3>
<pre><code class="byn">let b = move a
sum(move list)</code></pre>
<p><code>move</code> transfers ownership of a value that cannot be copied, such as a <code>List</code>; afterwards the source cannot be used. See <a href="memory.html">Memory and ownership</a>.</p>

<h2 id="optionals">Optionals</h2>

<h3 id="optional"><code>?</code> types and narrowing</h3>
<pre><code class="byn">let name: string? = null
if name is not null {
    print(name)          // `name` is a string here
}
let shown = name ?? "nobody"</code></pre>
<p><code>T?</code> is "a <code>T</code> or nothing"; plain types are never null. <code>is null</code> and <code>is not null</code> are the checks, and after <code>is not null</code> the value has its non-null type. <code>??</code> gives a fallback, <code>?.</code> stops at null (planned: not supported yet), and <code>x ?? return …</code> leaves early. <code>x == null</code> is rejected with a fix.</p>
<example name="reading-optional"/>

<h2 id="checked-types">Types that check for you</h2>

<h3 id="requires"><code>requires</code></h3>
<pre><code class="byn">struct Box {
    i32 w
        requires it &gt; 0
    i32 h
    requires w &lt; h at .w
}</code></pre>
<p>A <code>requires</code> clause is a rule a value must meet to exist. It is checked when the value is built, in every build mode. Building such a type can fail, so you write <code>try</code> or <code>catch</code>, and the error is <code>ValidationError&lt;Box&gt;</code>. A literal that breaks a rule is rejected at compile time. See <a href="features.html#requires">What makes Bayan different</a> for a complete program.</p>

<h3 id="units">Units</h3>
<note>Planned: not in the compiler yet. <code>unit</code> declarations do not parse today.</note>
<pre><code class="byn">unit Seconds(s): i64
unit Millis(ms) = Seconds / 1000

let t = 2s + 500ms             // Millis 2500</code></pre>
<p>A unit is a number type with a name and a literal suffix. Adding seconds to grams is a compile error. Conversion to a finer unit is automatic; to a coarser one names its rounding: <code>t.floor&lt;Seconds&gt;()</code>. A bare <code>0</code> never takes a unit. Units cost nothing at run time. <code>type distinct UserId = i64</code> is the same idea without arithmetic and works today: <code>type distinct Qty = i32 requires it &gt; 0</code>.</p>

<h2 id="attributes">Attributes and reasons</h2>

<h3 id="because"><code>@attr because "…"</code></h3>
<pre><code class="byn">@allow(BYN5102) because "the name matches the C API"
struct Header { … }

@json(expose) because "the client needs the key"
string apiKey

let s = x.trustUnchecked() because "demo input"

unsafe because "the C API wants a raw pointer" {
    …
}</code></pre>
<p><strong>Planned.</strong> Only the <code>trustUnchecked() because</code> line runs today; <code>@allow</code>, <code>@json(expose)</code> and <code>unsafe because</code> are in the specification but not the compiler. An attribute starts with <code>@</code>, as in <code>@derive(Json)</code>, and most take no reason. Anything that bypasses a safety rule, such as <code>@allow(…)</code>, <code>@json(expose)</code> on a <code>Secret</code> field, or other code, must say why in a <code>because</code> clause with a string. Without it the code does not compile.</p>
